NAME
       ssh - log in to another computer and run commands on it securely

SYNOPSIS
       ssh [-p PORT] [-i IDENTITY-FILE] [-l USER] [-o OPTION] [-q] [-t] [-v]
           [USER@]HOST [COMMAND]

DESCRIPTION
       ssh (the OpenSSH client) opens an encrypted connection to the sshd
       service on HOST and starts a shell there, or runs COMMAND and returns
       its output and exit status. Everything sent over the connection,
       passwords included, is encrypted.

       Without USER@, ssh logs in with the same user name you have on the
       local machine. Type exit (or press Ctrl+D) to close the remote shell and
       return to the local one. Watch the prompt: it shows which machine you
       are on.

   Host keys
       Each server has host keys that prove who it is. The first time you
       connect to a host, ssh shows the key's fingerprint and asks whether to
       go on; answer yes, and the key is saved in ~/.ssh/known_hosts. On later
       connections ssh checks that the server still has the same key and
       refuses to connect, with a loud warning, if it changed (a reinstalled
       server, or someone in the middle). When you know the change is
       expected, remove the old entry with ssh-keygen -R HOST.

   Authentication
       ssh tries public keys first: if you have a key pair (see ssh-keygen)
       and the public key is listed in ~/.ssh/authorized_keys of the remote
       account, you are logged in without the account's password (you may be
       asked for the key's passphrase). Otherwise the server asks for the
       password, if password logins are allowed.

OPTIONS
       -p PORT
              Connect to this port instead of 22.

       -i FILE
              Use this private key file instead of the defaults
              (~/.ssh/id_ed25519, ~/.ssh/id_ecdsa, ~/.ssh/id_rsa).

       -l USER
              Log in as USER (the same as USER@HOST).

       -o OPTION=VALUE
              Set an option the way ssh_config does, for example
              -o StrictHostKeyChecking=no, -o PasswordAuthentication=no
              (proves that a key login works) or -o BatchMode=yes (never ask
              questions; fail instead).

       -q     Quiet: do not print warnings and banners.

       -t     Ask for a terminal even when a command is given (for commands
              that need one, such as sudo asking for a password).

       -v     Verbose: print what ssh is doing, to find out why a login fails.

EXAMPLES
       Log in to server2 as student:

           ssh student@server2

       Run one command on server2 as root and come back:

           ssh root@server2 'systemctl is-active sshd'

       Check that a key login works without falling back to a password:

           ssh -o PasswordAuthentication=no student@server2 hostname

       Connect to an sshd that listens on port 2222:

           ssh -p 2222 student@server1.lab.example.com

FILES
       ~/.ssh/known_hosts
              Host keys of the servers you have connected to.

       ~/.ssh/id_ed25519, ~/.ssh/id_rsa ...
              Your private keys; the .pub files next to them are the public
              halves.

       ~/.ssh/config, /etc/ssh/ssh_config
              Client settings (see ssh_config(5)).

       ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
              On the server: the public keys that may log in to this account.

SEE ALSO
       scp(1), ssh-keygen(1), ssh-copy-id(1), ssh_config(5), sshd(8),
       sshd_config(5)
